+  "title": "GOV-0062: BADF-WP-GIT-I — shadow calibration on real BADF history: a recomputed `git-shadow` record (4/4 landings match, 14/14 identities conform, SOURCE_ADVANCED and RELEASE_BOUND replayed), gaps declared; root stays DESIGNED for GIT-J",
+  "problem": "## Observed\n\n`SHADOWED` means *the controls were run against real cases and behaved* (`docs/07`: representative tasks observed without affecting gates). `badf-research` and `badf-architecture` shadowed on real historical BADF records; `badf-solution-design` shadowed representatively and said so. `badf-git` has now shipped seven subskills (GIT-B…H), each proven failing-first and by mutation against **synthetic** scratch clones — and, unusually, the program **ran on itself**: every one of its work packages was integrated through the tools it was building. That history is the shadow corpus, and it is recomputable from the object store alone. Measured on `main` `605f97f`: **identity** — 14 of the last 14 first-parent landings carry a `BADF-WP-NNNN:` label and a canonical `Work-Package: WP-2026-NNNN` trailer with one NNNN (GIT-B's rule; the 10 landings after it landed had to, the 4 before happened to); **composition/landing** — of 15 landings since GIT-A, the 4 that carry a composition record (0076, 0079, 0080, 0081) have a landed content tree **equal** to the recorded `expected_content_tree`, and the 11 that predate GIT-E read `composition_verified: false` — visibly, never silently; **staleness** — a baseline at the GIT-F landing (`928fc6b`) judged at the GIT-G landing (`74282a4`) is `SOURCE_ADVANCED` (ancestor), the real forward case; **release** — `BADF-BASELINE-1.0.0 → 3f6119b` is `RELEASE_BOUND`; **the runner** — every CI run since GIT-C renders the inspectors on a detached checkout (`GIT_BASELINED … detached`, `CURRENT`, `EVIDENCE_ONLY`, `RELEASE_BOUND`). What real history **cannot** replay: the rewrite verdicts (`STALE_EVIDENCE`) — the rewritten heads were force-pushed away and are not on `origin` — and the recovery inventory on genuinely dirty state, observed locally during the program (`TOPIC RECOVERY_REQUIRED` before a push, `LOCAL` on a mid-rebase residue) but not committed. GTGM v0.1 §17 **GIT-I — Shadow: real BADF PR calibration**.\n\n## Expected\n\n**The shadow corpus becomes a committed, recomputed record — and its gaps are named, not implied.**\n\n1. **`examples/git-shadow-evidence.json`** — one `git-shadow` record listing every case with the verdict the tool renders: the 4 record-bearing landings (`wp`, `landed_as`, `expected_content_tree`, `landed_content_tree`, `MATCH`), the 11 landings without a record (`composition_verified: false`), the 14 identity-conforming landings (commit, label, trailer), the `SOURCE_ADVANCED` staleness case (`928fc6b → 74282a4`), the `RELEASE_BOUND` baseline, and the CI runner citations (run ids) for the detached-checkout inspectors — plus a **`non_coverage`** list: rewrite verdicts (synthetic only), the recovery inventory on real dirty state (observed, not committed), branch-name conformance of landed PR heads (observed through the GitHub API — branches are deleted on merge — not recomputable offline), and tag signing (none exist).\n2. **`tests/test_badf_git_shadow.py`** recomputes the record from history with the real tools — `content_tree` on each `landed_as` against the record read from the landed tree; the identity rule on each cited squash; `git_staleness` with a baseline taken at `928fc6b` and judged at `74282a4` in a scratch clone; `git_release_check` on the baseline — and fails if the committed record and history ever disagree. A case whose verdict cannot be recomputed offline is not allowed in `cases`; it belongs in `non_coverage`. This makes the shadow **self-verifying in CI and inside the composed world**, exactly like the other families' shadow examples.\n3. **`skills/badf-git/references/shadow-evidence.md`** — the measurement table (case · real history · what the contract had to represent · outcome), the honest first sentence about what is real and what is only synthetic, the runner citations, and **why the root stays `DESIGNED`**: the router's implementation *is* its seven `IMPLEMENTED` subskills and their failing-first/mutation suites; its shadow is this record; advancing it — the research precedent — is the operator's admission decision (GIT-J), never an agent's.\n4. **No registry status change, no lifecycle change, no new control, no new tool** — the shadow observes; it does not affect a gate. `badf-git` remains `DESIGNED`; the doctrine section records the calibration.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `examples/git-shadow-evidence.json` validates structurally (record kind, every case has `case`, the identifiers and a `verdict`; `non_coverage` non-empty) and every case's verdict is **recomputed** from history by the test module: 4/4 landings `MATCH`; 11/11 no-record landings have no record in their landed tree; 14/14 identity cases conform; the staleness case renders `SOURCE_ADVANCED` through `git_staleness`; the baseline renders `RELEASE_BOUND` through `git_release_check`.\n2. A tampered shadow record (a wrong tree, a wrong verdict, a case that cannot be recomputed) is refused by the tests (mutation of the example, not the code).\n3. `shadow-evidence.md` names every case class and every non-coverage item, states which verdicts were exercised only synthetically, and cites the CI run ids for the runner inspectors.\n4. `badf-git` root stays `DESIGNED`; no change to `badf/lifecycle.json`, `badf_gate.py`, `badf_compose.py`, schemas, workflow or authority; `repo` clean; this PR carries its own composition record; full suite + compose green in CI (the shadow tests run inside the composed world too).\n\n## Tests (`tests/test_badf_git_shadow.py`)\n\n- `test_shadow_record_is_well_formed_and_names_non_coverage`\n- `test_every_recorded_landing_matches_its_content_tree`\n- `test_every_no_record_landing_has_no_record_in_its_landed_tree`\n- `test_every_identity_case_conforms_on_real_history`\n- `test_staleness_case_renders_SOURCE_ADVANCED_with_the_real_tool`\n- `test_release_case_renders_RELEASE_BOUND_with_the_real_tool`\n- `test_tampered_shadow_record_is_refused`\n- `test_shadow_evidence_doc_names_every_case_and_gap_and_root_stays_designed`\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Advancing `badf-git` to `SHADOWED`/`APPROVED`/`ACTIVE` — **GIT-J**, the operator's admission decision.\n- Reconstructing force-pushed heads to replay rewrite verdicts (they are gone from `origin`; the synthetic proofs stand and the gap is declared).\n- Any new control, tool, schema, workflow step or authority; the remote-workspace mode (#133).\n\n## Risk / authority / stop conditions\n\n- Change class **C1** (evidence + tests + doc; no code path changes). Target gate **G07**, `HUMAN_REQUIRED`.\n- Stop if a case cannot be recomputed offline (it moves to non-coverage), or if honesty would require claiming a verdict history cannot support.\n- Lean mode: **enforced** — reuse the existing tools verbatim; no shadow-specific code paths.\n\n## Identity\n\n- Ledger **WP-2026-0082** · demand **BADF-DEM-0069** · this issue **GOV-0062** · phase label BADF-WP-GIT-I. Ids measured across `main`, every remote branch head and all issues.\n- Branch `wp/WP-2026-0082-git-shadow` in a linked worktree from `605f97f`; reconciles WP-2026-0081 (`composition_verified: true` — the fourth verified landing).\n- Sequence: A ✓ B ✓ C ✓ D ✓ E ✓ F ✓ G ✓ H ✓ → **I (this)** → J.\n\nsource: GTGM v0.1 §17 (GIT-I); `docs/07` lifecycle; the research/architecture/solution-design shadow precedents · discovered-by: BARCHI-2, the program that integrated itself through its own tools · status: PROPOSED\n\n🧠 **BARCHI-2** — plan posted; engineered under the operator's `/loop your goal on badf-git`.\n<!-- agent: architect -->\n",
